tags: sha256-hashes, sha256_hashes_per_sec

A score of one core: SHA-256 digests of 64-byte inputs per second.

The value is an integer, in hash per second. Example: 120000. More is
better. The node measures it with one process, so it is what one core does, in a guest
of the architecture of the announcement. The method is fixed: busybox 1.37.0 from the
image that the formal names by its digest, and the loop that the formal gives. A
faster method (for example a compiled program) is another benchmark.

The statistic is one run of at least 2 seconds. It is not a mean of runs and not a
percentile. A score that must be stable is another benchmark.

In an announcement, an absent score is not measured, never 0. In a requirement
(Resources.benchmarks), the node compares its score with "greater than or equal" at
each placement. A node that did not measure the score refuses.

=== formal
class=benchmark
clock=/proc/uptime of the guest, in hundredths of a second
method=busybox sha256sum of 256 copies of one 64-byte file (64 ASCII zeros) in each call; read the clock after each call; stop at the first read at or after 200 hundredths of a second
name=sha256-hashes
place=the benchmarks of a container or of an architecture
process=one process: the score is what one core does
score=floor(digests*100/elapsed hundredths of a second)
stat=one run on an idle guest: the rate over one window of at least 2 seconds; no mean of runs and no percentile
tools=busybox 1.37.0, the image index busybox:1.37.0@sha256:bdf57e528e45e4433820e045b29b4597825a1c9e38353532d90a01445013f82e, in a guest of the architecture
unit=hash/second
